import csv,json
from pathlib import Path
B=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
Q=list(csv.DictReader((B/'queue.csv').open(encoding='utf8',newline='')))
C=json.loads((B/'lead-clocks.json').read_text())
G=json.loads((B/'lead-geometry.json').read_text())
D={
'U001':dict(verdict='PROBABLE',period='23.20817',epoch='2339.50918',depth='275',raw='Seven fitted deep same-kind events in S38, S39, S65, S100, S101 and S102 have resolved symmetric contacts, with additional shallower dips offset by about 8 d. Raw SAP minima are 20–29% below local baseline. S38 and S39 contain adjacent orbital occurrences, anchoring the cycle directly; the seven-event fit has P=23.2081746 d and 0.00093 d unweighted timing residual RMS. The S12 near-edge feature is partial and was excluded from the clock.',timing='A half-period alias predicts an extra deep event in S38 at BTJD 2351.113 between observed S38/S39 occurrences. That actual S38 core has 36 quality-zero points, local baselines and -0.8 ppt median deficit versus about 275 ppt at the eclipse. The photometric period differs from Gaia SB1 P=23.2464098±0.0116866 d by 0.0382 d. An edge-on Gaia conjunction estimate is offset by -0.0973 phase at the fitted S38 epoch; the simple period-accumulation allowance alone is 0.97 d and full covariance is not available here. Thus the photometric binary is real, but tying this clock to the Gaia SB1 orbit is not secure.',source='S38 TESSCut difference flux peaks on the target WCS pixel with positive target-region baseline; this supports target-region origin at TESS resolution. SAP and KSPSAP preserve the principal dip; the helper pixel aperture is an alternate reduction of the same photons. Close unresolved blends are not excluded.',limits='Gaia orbital association and close-source ownership remain unresolved. Catalogue novelty is separately assessed by the shared catalogue worker.',next='Obtain spatially resolved eclipse photometry and a covariance-aware Gaia conjunction comparison.'),
'U002':dict(verdict='PLAUSIBLE',period='',epoch='',depth='',raw='S23 has one complete, flat-bottomed raw SAP depression centered by a local trapezoid at BTJD 1951.95793 with about 57 ppt depth and resolved ingress/egress. S50 has a broad wave but no matching discrete event.',timing='The current TESSCut inventory contains only S23 and S50, matching the frozen QLP inventory. Gaia P=23.128222 d predicts an S50 conjunction at 2668.933 BTJD, before quality-zero S50 begins at 2669.4; the following conjunction is after the sector ends. S50 therefore supplies no sensitive repeat null. One observed occasion cannot select an integer cycle or establish recurrence. A source-specific edge-on Gaia conjunction is within about 0.275 d of S23, supportive but not a confirmation.',source='S23 TESSCut difference peak is at the target WCS pixel. Raw SAP and KSPSAP show the same event; these use the same photons. The pixel check does not remove all close blends.',limits='Single event; no second sensitive QLP/TESSCut sector is currently available. Eclipse interpretation, source ownership and orbital cycle remain uncertain.',next='Obtain another complete eclipse with source localization.'),
'U003':dict(verdict='REFUTED',period='',epoch='',depth='',raw='The dips in S7, S34, S61, S87 and S88 sit in a repeating roughly 1.79 d train. Five independent local centers fit P=1.79159267 d, matching the cataloged EA neighbor TIC 412521403 at 30.57 arcsec and P=1.7916 d, rather than the target Gaia SB1 P=35.699912 d.',timing='The repeated short-period clock is directly present in raw SAP; a long Gaia-period selection of some windows is a cadence of this neighbor train, not evidence for a 35.7 d target eclipse. The selected events have no source-specific Gaia conjunction support.',source='The S34 TESSCut difference image peaks at the known EA neighbor position (about pixel 4.55,4.48), displaced from target WCS (3.52,3.42). The cataloged neighbor also has the matching exact short clock. This positively identifies contamination at TESS resolution.',limits='The selected dip interpretation as a target Gaia-binary eclipse is refuted; an unrelated weak target eclipse elsewhere is not excluded. The neighbor is a cataloged EB, not a new target discovery.',next='If target-specific eclipses are sought, use higher-resolution photometry separated from TIC 412521403.'),
'U004':dict(verdict='PROBABLE',period='',epoch='',depth='',raw='Two raw S22 dips at BTJD 1909.499 and 1922.893 recur in S49 at 2648.641 and 2662.034, respectively, with stable narrow morphology and about 23/29 ppt fitted depths. Each sector supplies both conjunction-like kinds; the separation repeats at 13.39 d. The two deep centers differ by 739.141 d.',timing='A conditional 25-cycle assignment gives P=29.56564 d, near Gaia SB1 P=29.54222 d. Other integers remain possible over the 739 d gap with only two observed sectors; therefore no adopted photometric period or precision error is reported. PRSA lists exact TIC 86182628 as an eclipsing-binary recovery with one-sector P=26.543965±0.200737 d, which is inconsistent with the conditional 25-cycle value and is not silently adopted. The source-specific edge-on Gaia conjunction differs by about 1.27 d from S22 deep center; the simple accumulated formal-period allowance is 0.79 d and full covariance remains untested.',source='S22/S49 raw SAP and corrected light curves retain the dips. TESSCut difference peaks near the target WCS pixel. This supports target-region source but not unique ownership against unresolved blends.',limits='Real repeating two-kind eclipses are supported; exact cycle count and Gaia orbital association remain unresolved. Exact TIC is a known PRSA EB recovery, independent of this physical-period uncertainty.',next='Obtain an intervening or newer event to eliminate integer aliases and refine Gaia conjunction timing.'),
'U005':dict(verdict='CONFIRMED',period='26.07966',epoch='1688.85990',depth='44',raw='Sixteen resolved raw SAP dips occur across S14/S15/S75/S76/S80–S83 in two nearly equal-depth conjunction tracks. Representative flat-bottom dips are 34–46 ppt in the long-cadence and later 10-minute data, with consistent contacts across years. A same-kind eight-event fit spanning 2019–2024 gives P=26.079664 d and 0.00462 d residual RMS. The two kinds alternate by about 13.14 and 12.94 d in both early and late sectors, compatible with eccentric-orbit conjunction spacing rather than a constant half-period.',timing='Successive same-kind events in adjacent S14/S15 and S75/S76 and repeated late sectors directly fix the approximate 26.08 d cycle. A P/2 single-kind interpretation fails because consecutive intervals are 13.1379/12.9402 d early and 13.1409/12.9387 d late. P/3, P/4 and P/6 predict complete raw S75 cores with 172–173 quality-zero points and median depths between -0.16 and +0.33 ppt against about 44 ppt actual eclipses. The first tested P/5 core is gapped (83 points), but an earlier P/5 prediction at BTJD 3352.753 has 173 complete points and -0.38 ppt median deficit. Gaia SB1 P=26.08509±0.00852 d agrees. An edge-on conjunction estimate is offset -0.0087 phase at the early epoch, within the 0.42 d simple period-accumulation allowance. Full Gaia covariance is not characterized.',source='The S81 TESSCut independent aperture/difference measurements for both conjunction kinds have nearly identical target-centered spatial patterns and positive target flux. Raw SAP and KSPSAP preserve representative dips. These are independent reductions of the same TESS photons, not additional observing occasions.',limits='The binary eclipse interpretation and target-region attribution satisfy this bounded review. TESS pixels do not uniquely exclude a very close unresolved blend. Catalogue novelty remains separate.',next='Fit both conjunction tracks jointly and obtain resolved imaging if available.'),
'U006':dict(verdict='CONFIRMED',period='46.54777',epoch='2409.54996',depth='230',raw='Four deep raw SAP eclipses in S40, S54, S80 and S81 have sharp matching ingress/egress and fitted depths about 228–241 ppt. Centers 2409.550, 2781.933, 3480.150 and 3526.696 BTJD fit cycle indices 0,8,23,24 with P=46.547769 d and 0.00081 d timing residual RMS. S80 begins near ingress, but the depression and recovery are clear; the other three provide complete context.',timing='The adjacent S80/S81 same-kind occurrences fix the approximate 46.55 d cycle independently of the long-baseline integer assignment. P/2 through P/5 predict S80 raw cores at BTJD 3503.424, 3495.666, 3491.787 and 3489.460: each has 120–121 quality-zero core points, both local baselines and median depths from -0.64 to +0.64 ppt against about 230 ppt actual eclipses. A P/6 prediction at 3487.908 is gapped, but the second P/6 prediction at 3495.666 has 121 complete points and -0.64 ppt median deficit. These are bounded actual-cadence nulls. Gaia SB1 P=46.549324±0.020222 d agrees, and a source-specific edge-on conjunction is within 0.0009 phase of the S40 measured event. The period error from a local trapezoid covariance is not a total calibrated ephemeris uncertainty.',source='S81 TESSCut difference flux peaks on target WCS, distinctly away from the 52.25 arcsec cataloged EA neighbor with P=0.6754534 d. The neighbor short clock cannot explain the observed 46.55 d train. S54 KSPSAP is badly distorted whereas raw SAP retains the eclipse; independent TESSCut supports the representative target event.',limits='Four target-region raw eclipses, independent reductions and compatible Gaia geometry satisfy the bounded physical classification. Close unresolved blends and detailed stellar parameters remain unmeasured. The distant cataloged EA is not this signal.',next='Obtain resolved follow-up photometry and a formal joint RV/eclipsing fit.'),
'U007':dict(verdict='REFUTED',period='',epoch='',depth='',raw='S56, S76 and S83 show many narrow deep and flanking shallower dips. Selected deep centers fit P=1.11451118 d across cycles 0,492,655, with a 0.00003 d residual RMS. This is the known short-period EB clock of TIC 331643515, 41.995 arcsec from the queued TIC 331643517. The neighbor table gives P_TESS=1.114507 d; its VSX P=1.67164 d is an alias.',timing='Selecting distant dips by Gaia P=53.251724 d confuses the 1.1145 d train with the spectroscopic target orbit. No measured target-owned same-kind 53.25 d eclipse clock follows from these events. S16 is sparse and does not rescue that association.',source='S56 TESSCut difference maximum at pixel (6,2) is displaced about two pixels from queued target WCS (3.71,3.47) and lies in the predicted direction of TIC 331643515 at (5.47,2.46). This positive spatial/clock match identifies the selected train as neighbor contamination. An IJspeert table separately has an exact queued TIC/Gaia row; that identity fact must not be conflated with ownership of this 1.1145 d train.',limits='The selected dips are not evidence that the queued Gaia SB1 is eclipsing. The exact-target IJspeert catalogue row requires its own interpretation; this review does not refute an independent target eclipse below the contaminated signal.',next='Separate both sources spatially and inspect the IJspeert target row and its underlying event train.'),
'U008':dict(verdict='PLAUSIBLE',period='',epoch='',depth='',raw='S24 shows one compact raw SAP depression near BTJD 1970.103, fitted about 21 ppt, with rapid recovery and a post-event overshoot. Other QLP sectors S16/S17/S57/S77/S83/S84 do not show a securely matching same-kind event. The S24 dip remains a singular candidate.',timing='At Gaia P=50.60815 d, a predicted S17 core at 1767.670 has 6/6 nominal 30-minute cadences, baseline 12+11 and median depth 0.1 ppt; S84 at 3589.564 has 51/52 core cadences and 0.3 ppt. These are local nulls at the nominal epoch, not phase-wide exclusions: Gaia period error 0.10017 d accumulates to about 0.4 d at S17 and 3.2 d at S84. The current TESSCut inventory adds S76 to the frozen QLP sectors, but its 5x5 target-region net flux is only about 0.4 e/s, so it is not a sensitive null.',source='S24 difference imagery is dominated by an approximately 30 e/s common background offset, preventing reliable pixel localization in the bounded cut. SAP and KSPSAP show the same photons, with the post-event overshoot requiring caution.',limits='One event with uncertain source and possible reduction/systematic alternative; recurrence and Gaia orbital link are unproven.',next='Acquire a second uninterrupted event and a valid target-flux cutout for localization.'),
'U009':dict(verdict='PROBABLE',period='52.14781',epoch='4247.12965',depth='70',raw='Current TESSCut inventory added S106–S109 beyond the frozen QLP S5/S32/S98 set. Two complete target-centered, steep-contact events in S106 and S108 fit BTJD 4247.12965 and 4299.27746, with independent centered-aperture depths about 73 and 69 ppt and durations about 0.28 d. Their separation is 52.14781 d. S98 QLP SAP and an alternate TESSCut aperture show ingress and a low interval near 4038.53, within about 0.01 d of four earlier cycles, but a 0.23 d gap removes full egress and post-gap baseline shifts; its trapezoid center is not used as a precision timing.',timing='S107 provides well-covered nulls at P/2 and P/3 aliases: 121 quality-zero core points at each trial, median local deficits -1.25, -0.85 and -0.40 ppt versus about 70 ppt at actual events. This bounded test supports a 52.15 d same-kind clock, while a longer period with alternating conjunctions is not exhaustively ruled out. Gaia SB1 P=52.13086±0.04613 d is compatible; an edge-on conjunction estimate is 0.91 d from S106, smaller than the roughly 3.4 d accumulated formal-period allowance from Gaia reference epoch.',source='S106 and S108 TESSCut difference images peak on the target WCS position in differently oriented sectors, within roughly 0.16–0.18 pixel of the target. S98 difference has a target-region component plus roughly 20 e/s common drift. QLP SAP and the independently made TESSCut aperture both show its partial event; these share TESS photons.',limits='Two full repeated events and a gap-truncated third support a real eclipsing source near the target. Close blending and the S98 background/reset prevent definitive source assignment and a precision long-baseline clock.',next='Acquire further target-centered repeats and a spatially resolved source check; compare complete Gaia RV covariance with eclipse timing.')
}
rows=[]
for q in Q:
 sid=q['sample_id'];tic=q['tic'];x=D[sid];d=B/tic
 novelty='KNOWN_PRIOR_EB' if sid in ('U004','U007') else 'NO_EXACT_MATCH_IN_CHECKED_SCOPE'
 ctx=json.loads((d/'lead-context.json').read_text());tim=json.loads((d/'lead-timing.json').read_text())
 sect=','.join(str(s['sector']) for s in ctx['sectors'])
 report=f"""# {sid} — TIC {tic}

**Physical verdict:** {x['verdict']} (provisional lead classification). **Bounded review:** COMPLETE. **Catalogue status:** {novelty}; no new-system claim is made here. The shared-catalogue receipts define the checked scope.

## Raw events and coverage

{x['raw']}

Cached QLP sector coverage: {sect}. Lead verified SHA256 of every used FITS and bulk-result file before measuring. `lead-context.json` contains cadence endpoints, quality-zero sample counts and flagged-window measurements; `lead-timing.json` contains local trapezoid measurements. The gallery `lead-raw-context.png` shows representative raw SAP alongside KSPSAP where present. Detector window centers were used only as search seeds.

## Timing and Gaia geometry

{x['timing']}

## Source and alternate reduction

{x['source']}

## Limits and next check

{x['limits']}

{x['next']}

## Provenance

`../queue.csv`, `../lead-source-hashes.json`, `../lead_context.py`, `../lead_timing.py`, `../lead-clocks.json`, `../lead-geometry.json`, helper pixel scripts/receipts/images and shared-catalogues exact target receipts form the audit trail. For current-sector additions see `../lead-current-sector-inventory.json`, `../lead-extra-sector-receipts.json`, and, for U009, `lead-new-clock.json` and `lead-alias-check.json`. Same photons in different reductions are not independent observations.
"""
 (d/'REPORT.md').write_text(report,encoding='utf8')
 rows.append(dict(sample_id=sid,tic=tic,sample_stratum=q['category'],physical_verdict=x['verdict'],full_review_completion='COMPLETE',novelty_status=novelty,period_days=x['period'],period_error_days='',primary_epoch_btjd=x['epoch'],depth_ppt=x['depth'],limitations=x['limits'].replace(' Catalogue novelty is separately assessed by the shared catalogue worker.','').replace(' Catalogue novelty remains separate.',''),next_check=x['next'],report=str((d/'REPORT.md').resolve())))
with (B/'RESULTS.csv').open('w',newline='',encoding='utf8') as f:
 w=csv.DictWriter(f,fieldnames=rows[0].keys());w.writeheader();w.writerows(rows)
print([(r['sample_id'],r['physical_verdict']) for r in rows])
